.TH ALESS 1 "2026-10-09" "aless 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.nh
.SH NAME
aless \- JSON, YAML, TOML, CSV, XML, INI, Markdown and more as one tree: a
jless\-style viewer in a terminal, and JSON on standard output for scripts and
agents
.SH SYNOPSIS
aless [OPTIONS] [FILE]...
.br
aless \-\-paths \-\-depth 1 FILE
.br
<command> | aless [OPTIONS]
.PP
\-h prints a summary of the options, and \-\-help this whole reference.
.SH DESCRIPTION
aless reads JSON, JSON Lines, JSON5, JSONC, jsonic, YAML, TOML, INI, CSV, TSV,
XML, ZON, Markdown and RSS or Atom feeds, and any line\-oriented format an ABNF
grammar describes, as one tree. In a terminal it shows the tree in a
jless\-style viewer, with tabs, panes, search and a watch mode that reloads a
changed file and keeps your place. Without one it prints JSON on standard
output for scripts and agents, and writes any of those formats as any other.
.SS "Without a screen"
aless prints instead of starting the viewer when an option listed under OUTPUT
OPTIONS is given, when standard output is not a terminal (a pipe, a file, an
agent\(aqs tool call), or when TERM=dumb, and it never waits for keys. A run
reads one input (\-\-check reads several), prints one answer on standard output
and exits 0, or prints {"error": {...}} on standard error and exits non\-zero:
see OUTPUT, ERRORS and EXIT STATUS. When the viewer cannot start for want of a
terminal, aless says so at once, before reading any input, with a usage error
and status 2.
.PP
For an agent, five rules:
.TP 7
\fB1.\fR
Pass an output option: \-\-json, \-\-paths, \-\-find, \-\-where, \-\-check,
\-\-render or \-\-alchemy. Never \-\-panes, which opens the viewer.
.TP 7
\fB2.\fR
Name the file as an argument. Standard input is read as JSON unless \-k FORMAT
says otherwise.
.TP 7
\fB3.\fR
Check the exit status before reading standard output: only 0 means it holds the
answer (\-\-check\(aqs report comes with 1 too).
.TP 7
\fB4.\fR
Quote a path for the shell: \-\-path \(aq.items[0]\(aq.
.TP 7
\fB5.\fR
Start small on a big or unknown file: \-\-paths \-\-depth 1, then \-\-path into
it.
.PP
aless \-\-generate skill prints an Agent Skill that teaches an agent all of
this.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Output options"
.TP 7
\fB\-\-json\fR
The document as JSON, or the value at the start that \-\-path or \-\-at gives:
what aless prints without a screen when no other output option is given.
Indented 2 spaces a level (\-\-indent N), or on one line with \-\-compact.
Numbers are 64\-bit floats, so an integer beyond 2\(ha53 comes out as the
nearest one, and NaN and the infinities as null; \-\-render json keeps each
number as the source spelled it.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-paths\fR
An entry for the start and for each node below it, in document order, in a
listing: {file, format, path, entries, total, limit, truncated} (see OUTPUT).
\-\-depth N goes N levels below the start, and \-\-limit N lists N entries at
most.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-find\fR \fIREGEX\fR
The entries of the nodes, the start and those below it, whose "key": value text
matches REGEX, in a listing with pattern and matches (see OUTPUT). It is the
viewer\(aqs search: case is ignored unless REGEX has a capital letter or ends
in /s, and [ ] { } match themselves unless escaped. \-\-depth and \-\-limit
apply as they do to \-\-paths.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-where\fR
The start\(aqs entry, with file and format: with \-\-at, the path a source
position is in; with \-\-path, the line and column a path starts at; with
neither, the root\(aqs.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-check\fR
Parse every FILE, or standard input when none is named, and report on each:
{ok, files: [{file, format, ok, error}]}, where a failing file\(aqs error is
the error object a run on that file alone prints. The one output option that
reads several inputs. Exit status 1 when any input fails, with the report still
on standard output. Takes no \-\-path or \-\-at.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-render\fR \fIFORMAT\fR
The value at the start written as FORMAT, streamed as the input is read: csv,
its records (the elements of the array at the start; the lines of JSON Lines;
the records of CSV and TSV) under a header row, every field quoted, CRLF line
ends; json, the value as \-\-json writes it, but with each number spelled as in
the source where that is JSON, and NaN and the infinities refused; or any
format whose crate carries a render. FORMAT is one of csv, ini, json, json5,
jsonc, jsonic, jsonl, markdown, toml, xml, yaml or zon. Every format but json
declares what it does not keep in a warning on standard error, on success too.
Takes \-\-path, not \-\-at. With \-\-alchemy it names the format the
program\(aqs table or JSON events are written as.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-alchemy\fR \fIFILE\fR
Run the alchemy program in FILE over the input, and stream what its export
answers: a text as it is, a table as CSV (\-\-render json: JSON records, an
object per row), JSON events as compact JSON (\-\-render csv: a table of them);
\-\-render FORMAT writes a table or JSON events as FORMAT. The program selects
what it reads, so no \-\-path, \-\-at or other output option goes with it. The
input reaches it as \-\-render reads it: JSON Lines, CSV and TSV a record at a
time.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-alchemy\-expr\fR \fITEXT\fR
\-\-alchemy, with the program\(aqs text on the command line: \-\-alchemy\-expr
\(aqdef export [input] input\(aq writes the document as JSON.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-explain\fR
With \-\-alchemy or \-\-alchemy\-expr, print the program\(aqs plan report as
one JSON object instead of running it, and read no input: the chain of calls,
the protocols, what is retained and under which limits, the ordering contract,
the renderer (the program\(aqs own default, whatever \-\-render names), and the
guarantee with its qualification. Run it first on a program you did not write.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-path\fR \fIPATH\fR
Start at PATH instead of the root, in the jq syntax every output prints (see
PATHS). Not with \-\-at, \-\-check or a program.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-at\fR \fILINE[:COL]\fR
Start at the node at that source position, counted from 1, columns in
characters (see POSITIONS). Not with \-\-path, \-\-check, \-\-render or a
program.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR
\-\-paths and \-\-find list at most N entries (default 200; 0 for all); total
and truncated say what was left out.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-compact\fR
JSON on one line: the answer of \-\-json, \-\-paths, \-\-find, \-\-where,
\-\-check, \-\-render json and \-\-explain, and an error or a warning on
standard error. A program\(aqs own JSON is on one line whatever is given.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-max\-output\fR \fISIZE\fR
Stop an \-\-alchemy program that writes more than SIZE (default 1G; 0 for no
limit): a transduce error, RESOURCE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED naming max_output_bytes,
status 5. SIZE is bytes, or a whole number with K, M or G (1024s).
.SS "Options for both the output and the viewer"
.TP 7
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-kind\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fIFORMAT\fR
Parse every input as FORMAT instead of by its extension: a format\(aqs name
(json, jsonl, jsonic, jsonc, json5, yaml, toml, ini, csv, tsv, xml, zon,
markdown, feed or text), one of its extensions (yml, md, ndjson), or a
\-\-grammar NAME. Standard input is read as JSON unless this says otherwise.
\-\-format is another name for this option.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-grammar\fR \fINAME=FILE\fR
Read a file whose extension or whole name is NAME, in any case (x.hosts,
/etc/hosts), with the ABNF grammar in FILE, which is read within \-\-max\-size
and compiled within \-\-timeout before any input is read. NAME is then a
format, as \-k takes it and every output reports it; NAME,NAME2=FILE gives it
two names, and the option repeats. See CUSTOM GRAMMARS.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-grammar\-expr\fR \fINAME=ABNF\fR
\-\-grammar, with the grammar\(aqs text on the command line: everything after
the first =.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR
\-\-paths and \-\-find go at most N levels below the start (default: every
level). The viewer folds the containers deeper than N levels when it opens a
file.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-indent\fR \fIN\fR
Indent \-\-json and \-\-render json N spaces a level, and the viewer\(aqs tree
(default 2; at most 16).
.TP 7
\fB\-\-max\-size\fR \fISIZE\fR
Refuse an input larger than SIZE (default 64M; 0 for no limit), and a
\-\-grammar or \-\-alchemy file too, with a too_large error, status 5: a parse
takes about 40 bytes of memory per byte of input. SIZE is bytes, or a whole
number with K, M or G (1024s). JSON Lines, CSV and TSV that \-\-render or
\-\-alchemy streams are read a record at a time, and the limit does not apply
to them.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fISECONDS\fR
Stop a parse, or a \-\-grammar compile, that runs longer than SECONDS (2.5,
90s, 2m; default none; 0 for no limit), with a timeout error, status 6, that
says how far it got. Under \-\-render and \-\-alchemy it covers the whole run.
Without a screen, on standard input, the time runs from the start, so waiting
on the input counts. A parse runs at about a megabyte a second: a caller with a
deadline of its own should pass one a few seconds shorter.
.SS "Viewer options"
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-watch\fR
Do not reload a file when it changes.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-watch\fR
Reload a file when it changes, keeping your place (the default).
.TP 7
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-mode\fR \fIMODE\fR
Start in data mode (the default), the streamlined tree, or in line mode, every
line of a pretty\-printed rendering; m switches between them.
.TP 7
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-line\-numbers\fR
Show absolute line numbers.
.TP 7
\fB\-N\fR, \fB\-\-no\-line\-numbers\fR
Hide absolute line numbers.
.TP 7
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-relative\-line\-numbers\fR
Show line numbers relative to the focused row.
.TP 7
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-no\-relative\-line\-numbers\fR
Hide relative line numbers.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-scrolloff\fR \fIN\fR
Keep N rows around the focus when scrolling (default 3).
.TP 7
\fB\-\-hidden\fR
Show dot\-files in the file explorer.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-ascii\fR
Draw fold markers with ASCII characters (v and >).
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR, \fB\-\-no\-colour\fR
Draw without colours, as when NO_COLOR is set.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-no\-mouse\fR
Leave the mouse to the terminal rather than capture it.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-panes\fR \fIPANES\fR
Open panes beside the input: out, the document as \-\-render or \-\-alchemy
writes it (JSON when neither is given), and program, the program; out,program
opens both. With \-\-panes, \-\-render and \-\-alchemy choose the output
pane\(aqs content instead of printing, the other output options are refused,
and so is a run without a terminal (status 2). C\-w moves between the panes, s
shows a pane\(aqs text or its tree, and :pane out|program|close opens or closes
one.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-stacked\fR
Stack the panes rather than place them side by side.
.SS "Other options"
.TP 7
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a summary of the options (\-h), or this reference (\-\-help).
.TP 7
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version: aless 0.1.0.
.TP 7
\fB\-\-generate\fR \fIWHAT\fR
Print a file made from this reference: man, the man page (aless.1);
complete\-bash, complete\-zsh, complete\-fish or complete\-powershell, that
shell\(aqs completions; or skill, the Agent Skill (SKILL.md) that teaches an
agent to drive aless. See FILES.
.SH "INPUT AND FORMATS"
FILE is a path, or \- for standard input. Without a FILE, aless reads standard
input when it is not a terminal; otherwise output fails with a usage error, and
the viewer explores the current directory. Every output option but \-\-check
reads one input: run aless once per file, or \-\-check several. A directory is
a usage error without a screen, and opens the file explorer in the viewer.
Options end at \-\-, after which every argument is a FILE, and a long
option\(aqs value may follow it or be attached to it (\-\-kind=yaml).
.PP
The format comes from a file\(aqs extension, or from its whole name for a
\-\-grammar NAME (/etc/hosts), and \-k FORMAT overrides it for every input. A
file whose extension no format claims is read as plain text, an array of its
lines. Map keys keep their source order. The formats, with the extensions that
imply each:
.TP 10
\fBjson\fR
json geojson har jsonld webmanifest
.PD 0
.TP 10
\fBjsonl\fR
jsonl ndjson
.TP 10
\fBjsonic\fR
jsonic
.TP 10
\fBjsonc\fR
jsonc (trailing commas accepted)
.TP 10
\fBjson5\fR
json5
.TP 10
\fByaml\fR
yaml yml
.TP 10
\fBtoml\fR
toml
.TP 10
\fBini\fR
ini cfg conf cnf
.TP 10
\fBcsv\fR
csv (records keyed by the header row)
.TP 10
\fBtsv\fR
tsv tab (as csv)
.TP 10
\fBxml\fR
xml svg xhtml xsd xsl xslt plist
.TP 10
\fBzon\fR
zon
.TP 10
\fBmarkdown\fR
md markdown (its syntax tree)
.TP 10
\fBfeed\fR
rss atom (normalised to an Atom shape)
.TP 10
\fBtext\fR
txt text log (and every extension no format claims)
.PD
.SH "PATHS"
\-\-path takes jq\(aqs syntax, which every output prints, so a path can go
straight back in: ., .a.b[0], ."odd key", .["a.b"], .[0] for a root array\(aqs
first item and .[\-1] for its last. Also accepted: a.b[0] and [0] without the
leading dot, JSONPath\(aqs $.a[\(aqb\(aq][0], and JSON Pointer\(aqs /a/b/0.
There are no wildcards, slices or recursive descent: pipe \-\-json into jq for
those. Quote a path for the shell, whose globbing would take [0]. Under
\-\-render, [\-1] on an array is a usage error, since a stream cannot count
from the end; on an object it is the key \-1, as everywhere.
.SH "POSITIONS"
\-\-at takes LINE or LINE:COL, counted from 1, columns in characters, as an
entry\(aqs line and col are. Inside the text, \-\-at answers the node starting
last at or before the position on its line, the innermost of several starting
there (a line alone, or a column before the line\(aqs first node, that first
node; on a line with no node of its own, a comment or a closing bracket, the
last node before the line, or the document\(aqs first node when none is).
Outside the text it names nothing, status 4: a line past the last line, or a
column past the end of its line, where a line\(aqs text excludes its terminator
(LF or CRLF), a trailing terminator starts no line, and an empty line has no
column inside it.
.SH "OUTPUT"
An entry describes one node:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
{"path":".spec.replicas","kind":"number","line":12,"col":3,"value":3}
.fi
.RE
.TP 11
\fBpath\fR
where it is, in jq syntax: give it back to \-\-path, or to jq, unchanged
.TP 11
\fBkind\fR
object, array, string, number, boolean or null
.TP 11
\fBline, col\fR
where the node starts, from 1, columns in characters: at its key when it has
one, else at its value. Exact for the JSON family, TOML, INI, CSV and ZON,
best\-effort for YAML, XML and Markdown, and null when unknown
.TP 11
\fBlength\fR
a container\(aqs item count, or the full length of a string cut short
.TP 11
\fBvalue\fR
a scalar\(aqs value. A string over 200 characters is cut to 200, with
"truncated": true; NaN and the infinities are "NaN", "Infinity" and
"\-Infinity"
.PP
What each option prints on standard output when it succeeds:
.TP 17
\fB\-\-json\fR
the value itself, indented 2 spaces a level (\-\-indent N), or on one line
(\-\-compact)
.TP 17
\fB\-\-paths\fR
{file, format, path, entries: [entry, ...], total, limit, truncated}
.TP 17
\fB\-\-find\fR
{file, format, path, pattern, matches: [entry, ...], total, limit, truncated}
.TP 17
\fB\-\-where\fR
{file, format, ...entry}: the entry, with file and format first
.TP 17
\fB\-\-check\fR
{ok, files: [{file, format, ok, error}]}, error null for a file that parses
.TP 17
\fB\-\-render csv\fR
CSV: a header row, then a record per row, every field quoted, CRLF line ends
.TP 17
\fB\-\-render json\fR
the value, indented as \-\-json is, each number as the source spelled it where
that is JSON
.TP 17
\fB\-\-render FORMAT\fR
the document in FORMAT, in its always\-quoted profile, so that nothing reads
back as another kind
.TP 17
\fB\-\-alchemy\fR
what the program exports: a text as it is, a table as CSV, JSON events as
compact JSON
.TP 17
\fB\-\-explain\fR
{entry, output, protocol, chain, retention, renderer, guarantee, qualification,
\&...}
.PP
file is the path as given, or \- for standard input; format is what the input
was read as; path is where the listing starts. total counts every entry the
listing found, and truncated is true when that is more than \-\-limit let
through: raise \-\-limit (0 for all), or narrow with \-\-path or \-\-depth.
.PP
A \-\-render that succeeds, in any format but json, also writes a warning on
standard error that says what the format does not keep: {"warning": {"kind":
"loss", "message", "file", "render", "loss": [sentence, ...]}}, with adapter
naming the inferred table or records when one stood between the shapes. Status
0 is success whatever standard error holds.
.SH "ERRORS"
An error is one JSON object on standard error, and standard output is then
empty, with two exceptions: a failed \-\-check still prints its report, and a
\-\-render or \-\-alchemy stream that fails leaves what it had written, every
record whole and none in part (but for a record over 16 MB, and another
format\(aqs render, such as \-\-render yaml, which can stop inside one). A
parse error:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
{"error": {"kind": "parse", "file": "bad.json", "format": "json",
  "code": "unexpected", "message": "unexpected end of input",
  "line": 2, "col": 1, "hint": "The document ends before it is ...",
  "source_line": "", "report": "[tabnas/unexpected]: unexpected ..."}}
.fi
.RE
.PP
kind says what failed, and which fields come with it. A parse error\(aqs fields
are always there, null where they do not apply; another kind\(aqs fields named
"when" are there only then:
.TP 11
\fBparse\fR
the input did not parse: file, format, code (the grammar\(aqs: unexpected,
unterminated_string, too_deep, ...), message, line, col, hint, source_line (the
line the error is on) and report (the whole report the viewer shows,
uncoloured)
.TP 11
\fBio\fR
an input, a \-\-grammar file or a program file could not be read, or the output
not written: a parse error\(aqs fields, with the code io (file null when it was
standard output)
.TP 11
\fBtoo_large\fR
over \-\-max\-size: an io error\(aqs fields, plus size (null for standard
input, which is read no further) and limit, in bytes; the hint names the
\-\-max\-size that would read it
.TP 11
\fBtimeout\fR
past \-\-timeout: a parse error\(aqs fields, line and col showing how far the
parse got (null when the input was still being read, or the parse finished
late; a read a record at a time names the line its record starts on, col null),
plus seconds
.TP 11
\fBnot_found\fR
\-\-path or \-\-at names nothing: file, format, message, the path or at given,
nearest (the entry of the deepest node the path reached, or of the node a
position inside the text would have answered) and keys (that node\(aqs first
keys when it is an object)
.TP 11
\fBusage\fR
the command is wrong: message, and nothing more, but that a \-\-grammar that
does not compile adds grammar, and file when it came from one
.TP 11
\fBtransduce\fR
a \-\-render or \-\-alchemy stream failed: code (INPUT_INVALID,
RESOURCE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED, OUTPUT_FAILED, DUPLICATE_MEMBER,
TARGET_VALUE_UNREPRESENTABLE, ...), message, file, format, then path, limit
({name, value}), line, col and hint when the failure has them, and output:
"partial" when some of the result had been written, else "none". One a program
raised with a position of its own has file, line and col the program\(aqs,
format null, and input, the document\(aqs name
.TP 11
\fBalchemy\fR
the program is wrong: code (DSL_PARSE_ERROR, DSL_TYPE_ERROR, STREAM_REUSED,
STREAMABILITY_UNKNOWN), message led by a finer code (unbalanced, unknown_name,
arity, ...), file (the program\(aqs path, or \-\-alchemy\-expr), format null,
line and col in the program, output, and input (the document\(aqs name) once
the input was open
.PP
An io, too_large or timeout error about a \-\-grammar file adds grammar, with
file the grammar file and format null. An error met while \-\-render was
writing (transduce, parse, timeout, or the render\(aqs own alchemy one) adds
loss, the sentences its warning gives on success: an empty list for json.
\-\-compact puts an error on one line. The README\(aqs "Scripts and agents"
section has every case at length.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP 7
\fB0\fR
success: standard output holds the answer
.TP 7
\fB1\fR
the input did not parse (parse); with \-\-check, an input failed, and the
report says which; with \-\-render or \-\-alchemy, the input or its records
will not do (transduce: INPUT_INVALID, and the other input, protocol and target
codes)
.TP 7
\fB2\fR
bad usage (usage): an unknown option, a bad path, no input, a directory, a
\-\-grammar or an \-\-alchemy program that does not compile (alchemy), or the
viewer without a terminal
.TP 7
\fB3\fR
an input, a \-\-grammar file or an \-\-alchemy program file could not be read,
or the output not written (io; transduce OUTPUT_FAILED)
.TP 7
\fB4\fR
\-\-path or \-\-at names nothing (not_found)
.TP 7
\fB5\fR
an input, a \-\-grammar file or an \-\-alchemy program file is over
\-\-max\-size (too_large); with \-\-render or \-\-alchemy, over a limit of the
transducer\(aqs, a program\(aqs output over \-\-max\-output among them
(transduce RESOURCE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED)
.TP 7
\fB6\fR
a parse, or a \-\-grammar compile, ran past \-\-timeout, or the input was still
being read when it passed; with \-\-render or \-\-alchemy, the whole run, the
program\(aqs work included (timeout)
.SH "LARGE INPUTS AND STREAMING"
An input is read whole and parsed whole before anything is printed, at about a
megabyte a second and 40 bytes of memory per byte of input. \-\-max\-size
(default 64M) refuses a larger input before it is read, and \-\-timeout stops a
parse that runs too long with an error that says how far it got: a caller with
a deadline of its own should pass a \-\-timeout a few seconds shorter. \-\-path
and \-\-depth shrink the output, not the parse. A reader that stops early
(aless \-\-json big.json | head) ends aless quietly, with status 0.
.PP
\-\-render and \-\-alchemy stream instead. JSON Lines, CSV and TSV are read a
record at a time, whatever their size, so \-\-max\-size does not apply to them
(a record over 64 MB fails); the JSON family, jsonic, YAML, ZON and Markdown
write their records as the parse proceeds, and the other formats after it. A
document a grammar refuses to stream part\-way is read whole and written all
the same when nothing has been written yet; otherwise the error says output
"partial".
.PP
A document nested deeper than aless reads fails as a parse error with the code
too_deep: past about 1,000 levels, or sooner where the grammar has a limit of
its own (127 levels for JSON, JSONL, JSONic, JSON5, YAML, TOML, INI and ZON,
256 for XML, 512 for JSONC).
.PP
Numbers are 64\-bit floats: \-\-json and entries give an integer beyond 2\(ha53
as the nearest one, and \-\-json writes NaN and the infinities as null.
\-\-render json keeps a number as the source spelled it where that is JSON, and
refuses NaN and the infinities (transduce TARGET_VALUE_UNREPRESENTABLE, status
1).
.SH "CUSTOM GRAMMARS"
\-\-grammar NAME=FILE reads the files whose extension or whole name is NAME, in
any case, with the ABNF grammar in FILE (RFC 5234, compiled by tabnas/abnf),
and \-k NAME reads any input with it. ; @object a b and ; @array comments on a
rule say what it builds; without them the value is the compiler\(aqs parse
tree, a {"rule", "src", "kids"} node per rule. The input is read as plain text:
{ } [ ] : , are ordinary characters, # starts a comment to the end of the line,
and a word is the token TX (NR, ST and VL bring numbers, quoted strings and
true, false and null back). A grammar that does not compile is a usage error
before any input is read, and so is a repetition count over 1,024, or
repetitions that would have the compiler write more than 1,024 rules. An input
the grammar does not accept is a parse error, with format the grammar\(aqs
name.
.PP
Grammars for /etc/hosts, crontabs, /etc/passwd, /etc/group, /etc/fstab,
/etc/resolv.conf and KEY=value files, each with a sample and a guide to writing
one, are in aless\(aqs source:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
https://github.com/rjrodger/aless/tree/main/tests/fixtures/grammars
.fi
.RE
.SH "THE VIEWER"
In a terminal each FILE opens in a tab of its own, watched and reloaded when it
changes, keeping your place. A directory opens in the file explorer, where
Enter opens a file. Without a FILE the viewer reads standard input when it is
not a terminal, and otherwise explores the current directory. The keys are
jless\(aqs, and F1 or :help inside aless lists them all; the ones used most:
.TP 10
\fBj k\fR
down / up; a count before a key repeats it (3j)
.PD 0
.TP 10
\fBh l\fR
collapse / expand, or go to the parent / first child
.TP 10
\fBJ K\fR
next / previous sibling
.TP 10
\fBg G\fR
first / last row; Ng goes to row N
.TP 10
\fBC\-d C\-u\fR
half a page down / up
.TP 10
\fBSpace\fR
toggle the focused container
.TP 10
\fBc e\fR
collapse / expand the focused node and its siblings
.TP 10
\fB/ ?\fR
search forward / backward; n N for the next / previous match
.TP 10
\fByy yp yq\fR
copy the value / its path / its jq path
.TP 10
\fBm\fR
switch between data mode and line mode
.TP 10
\fBs\fR
show the source text at the focused node
.TP 10
\fBTab\fR
the next tab (Shift\-Tab the previous)
.TP 10
\fB:\fR
a command: :open PATH, :format FORMAT, :w FILE, :help
.TP 10
\fBq\fR
close the tab, quitting with the last; C\-c quits
.PD
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP 10
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
set and not empty: no colours, as \-\-no\-color
.TP 10
\fBTERM\fR
dumb: print instead of starting the viewer, as when standard output is not a
terminal, since such a terminal cannot draw it
.SH "FILES"
A release archive carries the man page and the completions, which \-\-generate
also prints:
.TP 18
\fBman/aless.1\fR
the man page (\-\-generate man)
.PD 0
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/aless.bash\fR
bash (\-\-generate complete\-bash)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/_aless\fR
zsh (\-\-generate complete\-zsh)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/aless.fish\fR
fish (\-\-generate complete\-fish)
.TP 18
\fBcompletions/_aless.ps1\fR
PowerShell (\-\-generate complete\-powershell)
.PD
.PP
A shell reads its completions from where it looks for them (a package puts them
there), or from its startup file each time it starts:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
eval "$(aless \-\-generate complete\-bash)"     # \(ti/.bashrc
eval "$(aless \-\-generate complete\-zsh)"      # \(ti/.zshrc, after compinit
aless \-\-generate complete\-fish | source      # config.fish
.fi
.RE
.PP
The Agent Skill (\-\-generate skill) is a file an agent loads from a directory
of its own. For Claude Code:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
mkdir \-p \(ti/.claude/skills/aless
aless \-\-generate skill > \(ti/.claude/skills/aless/SKILL.md
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.nf
aless \-\-paths \-\-depth 1 config.yaml       what is in it
aless \-\-json \-\-path \(aq.spec.containers[0]\(aq deploy.yaml
aless \-\-where \-\-at 42:7 deploy.yaml       the path at a linter\(aqs 42:7
aless \-\-where \-\-path .a.b config.yaml     the line a path is on
aless \-\-find \(aq"image":\(aq deploy.yaml       every image, with its line
aless \-\-check $(git ls\-files \(aq*.toml\(aq)    do they all parse?
aless \-k csv \-\-json < data.csv            stdin is JSON unless \-k says
aless \-\-render csv \-\-path .items x.json   the records as CSV
aless \-\-render json big.yaml              the document as JSON
aless \-\-render yaml data.csv              any format as any other
aless \-\-alchemy export.alc api.json       a program\(aqs output
aless \-\-alchemy export.alc \-\-explain      what the program will do
aless \-\-grammar hosts=hosts.abnf \-\-json /etc/hosts
.fi
